# Fullstack MCP POC

This workspace is a proof of concept for Figma-driven fullstack generation where frontend and backend output are coordinated from one parent-level Claude orchestrator.

![Fullstack architecture diagram](docs/architecture-diagram.svg)

## Stack Overview

| Layer | Path | Status | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Frontend | `nextjs-base/` | Existing | Next.js 16.1.3 + React 19 app for reusable blocks, screens, and page composition |
| Backend | `strapi-base-v5/` | Existing | Strapi v5.36.1 app for blocks, elements, dynamic zones, and content modeling |
| Orchestrator | Parent directory | Planned | Single source generation layer that reads Monday items, fetches Figma context, breaks large screens into blocks, and dispatches FE + BE generation |

## Objective

The goal is to make the parent-level Claude orchestrator the only intake point for design-to-code work.

Each Monday.com item assigned to the developer should be enough to trigger:

1. task intake from Monday.com
2. Figma extraction from the linked frame or screen
3. screen-to-block decomposition when the task is too large
4. coordinated frontend generation in `nextjs-base`
5. coordinated backend schema generation in `strapi-base-v5`
6. QA, verification, and status sync back to Monday.com

## Current Repo Signals

The repository already contains app-level Claude workflows that make the orchestration approach viable:

- `nextjs-base/.claude/commands/figma.md` handles Figma-to-Next.js component generation
- `strapi-base-v5/.claude/commands/figma.md` handles Figma-to-Strapi block generation
- `nextjs-base/ai-memory.md` already stores scanned frontend context for reuse decisions
- both apps include `.claude/skills/*` and `QA-monday-push` prompts tied to Figma and Monday workflows
- root `.claude` commands can also drive Playwright-based page validation against Figma after page seeding
- root skills now also define reusable company generation standards so future runs reuse shared UI primitives, Strapi patterns, and server-first data-fetching conventions

This means the missing piece is the root orchestrator and shared task planning layer, not the app-specific generation logic.

## Target Architecture

### 1. Intake

- Monday.com is the work queue
- each assigned item must include the relevant Figma URL
- the orchestrator reads only the items assigned to the current developer

### 2. Analysis

- the orchestrator reads the Figma frame or screen through Figma MCP
- it decides whether the item is a single reusable block or a full screen
- when the item is a full screen, the orchestrator splits it into smaller blocks for better reuse, pixel accuracy, and output quality

### 3. Planning

- a shared work manifest is created at the root level
- the manifest records the Monday item, Figma metadata, detected sections, FE targets, BE targets, dependencies, and verification checklist

### 4. Execution

- FE generation runs inside `nextjs-base` using the existing Figma-oriented Claude commands and skills
- BE generation runs inside `strapi-base-v5` using the existing Strapi Figma generator and schema conventions
- both sides must reuse current components, tokens, blocks, and elements before creating anything new

### 5. Verification

- FE checks visual fidelity, responsiveness, accessibility, and token reuse
- BE checks schema validity, reuse discipline, field modeling quality, and dynamic-zone registration
- the orchestrator consolidates outputs into a single delivery unit

### 6. Reporting

- progress, QA notes, and final delivery status are pushed back to Monday.com
- if one assigned item is decomposed into multiple sections, the orchestrator can report per-block status under the same task

## Single Source Generation Model

| Input | Orchestrator Action | Frontend Result | Backend Result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Single block with Figma URL | Generate directly | Reusable component or block | Matching Strapi block or element |
| Full screen with Figma URL | Decompose before generation | Section components plus composed screen | Multiple reusable elements or blocks plus dynamic-zone wiring |
| Existing screen update | Diff against current implementation | Patch existing components | Patch existing schemas |
| Content-only change | Minimize UI regeneration | Limited FE change or no-op | Content model or content update |

## Screen Decomposition Rules

When the assigned task is a complete screen, the orchestrator should split it into blocks such as:

- hero or banner
- content sections
- card grids or repeaters
- forms
- media modules
- listings
- CTA sections
- shared layout areas like headers or footers

Recommended rules:

- isolate repeatable content into reusable Strapi elements
- keep page composition at the block or dynamic-zone layer
- keep frontend wrappers reusable and route-agnostic where possible
- reuse tokens and shared UI before introducing new variants
- prefer smaller verifiable units over one large generation pass

